[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Data Binding and Observables in NativeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Data binding is a core concept in NativeScript that connects your User Interface (UI) components to your application logic. When data changes in your underlying model, the UI automatically updates to reflect those changes, and vice versa."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Observables"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"At the heart of NativeScript's data binding system is the Observable class. An Observable wraps your data and notifies the UI whenever a property value changes. To use it, you must import it from the '@nativescript/core' module."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"import { Observable } from '@nativescript/core';

export class MyViewModel extends Observable {
    constructor() {
        super();
        this.set('message', 'Hello, NativeScript!');
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Types of Data Binding"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"One-Way Binding: Data flows from the model to the view. If the model changes, the UI updates."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Two-Way Binding: Data flows both ways. User input in the UI (like typing in a TextField) automatically updates the model property."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Binding in XML"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In your XML layout files, you use binding expressions enclosed in square brackets for properties and curly braces for text content. Here is a practical example showing a Label and a TextField bound to the same observable property."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"<Page loaded="onPageLoaded" xmlns="http://schemas.nativescript.org/tns.xsd">
    <StackLayout class="p-20">
        <Label text="{{ message }}" class="h2 mb-20" />
        <TextField text="{{ message }}" class="input" />
    </StackLayout>
</Page>"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always extend the Observable class or use the fromObject utility function to make your data reactive."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep your business logic inside your ViewModel classes rather than your page loading scripts to maintain a clean MVVM architecture."}]
